1. c4 c5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. g3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. Bg2 Nc7 6. b3 e5 7. Bb2 Be7 8. Rc1
O-O 9. Na4 Nd7 10. Nf3 f6 11. Qc2 Ne6 12. Nh4 Nb6 13. Nf5 Nxa4 14. bxa4 Rb8 15.
f4 exf4 16. gxf4 Re8 17. Rg1 Bf8 $2 (17... Kh8 {stops all the forthcoming
nonsense.}) 18. Bc6 {"White starts on a career of joyous adventure" (Yorkshire
Post columnist)} Nd4 19. Nh6+ Kh8 20. Nf7+ Kg8 21. Qc4 ({Some versions of this
game have White repeating to gain time with} 21. Nh6+ Kh8 22. Nf7+ Kg8) 21...
Rxe2+ 22. Qxe2 Nxe2 23. Nxd8 Nxg1 $2 (23... bxc6 24. Kxe2 Rxb2 25. Rb1 Rxa2 26.
Rb8 {"with an easy win," wrote Reuben Fine in his book 'The World's Greatest
Chess Games'. Maybe a win, but 'easy'?...} Bf5 27. Nxc6 Be4 28. Rc8 Rxa4 {
and the win starts to look quite difficult as the knight doesn't have a
convincing exit strategy.}) 24. Bd5+ Kh8 25. Nf7+ Kg8 26. Kf2 Nh3+ 27. Kg3 b5
28. axb5 Bb7 29. Bc4 a6 30. a4 axb5 31. axb5 Nxf4 32. Kxf4 h5 33. Rg1 Kh7 34.
Bxf6 {Sources: Yorkshire Post and Leeds Intelligencer, Thursday 8 January 1931;
Sunday Times, 1 March 1931; (18-end) BCM, February 1931, p65} (34. Bxf6 gxf6
35. Bd3+ {mates.}) 1-0

